<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-overline">Section 6</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Nine decades of data reveal a category that has evolved from a studio-system formality
      into a genuinely global, cross-genre recognition of compositional excellence.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaway-grid">
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-num">01</div>
        <p>
          <strong>Repeat dominance is rare and getting rarer — except at the very top.</strong>
          Only 4 of 79 composers (5%) have won three or more times, yet those four composers
          account for 14 of 93 wins (15%). The gap between the elite repeat winners and the
          broad base of one-time winners is widening: Ludwig Göransson's concentrated 4-win
          run in the 2020s echoes the Tiomkin/Steiner dominance of the 1950s, suggesting a
          pendulum swing back toward concentration after the diffuse 2000s.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-num">02</div>
        <p>
          <strong>The category has globalized decisively.</strong> In the Golden Age (1934–1959),
          every winner was American or European. The Contemporary era (2000–2026) has recognized
          composers from Iceland, India, China, Argentina, Mexico, and beyond. This reflects both
          the Academy's expanded membership and the film industry's increasingly international
          production landscape.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-num">03</div>
        <p>
          <strong>Genre boundaries have dissolved.</strong> Early winners were nearly all
          orchestral scores for dramatic or epic films. The last three decades have seen wins
          for electronic scores (Moroder, Reznor/Ross), hip-hop-inflected work (<em>Black Panther</em>),
          jazz-influenced composition (<em>Round Midnight</em>, <em>Soul</em>), and ambient/minimalist
          approaches (Santaolalla, <em>The Brutalist</em>). The category now rewards compositional
          excellence regardless of instrumentation or tradition.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-num">04</div>
        <p>
          <strong>Collaborative wins are re-emerging.</strong> After the Golden Age's multi-composer
          entries (reflecting studio department credits), solo winners dominated from the 1960s through
          the 2000s. The last two decades have seen a resurgence of duo and trio wins — Reznor/Ross,
          the <em>Soul</em> trio, the <em>Last Emperor</em> trio — reflecting modern scoring's
          increasingly collaborative production model.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-num">05</div>
        <p>
          <strong>Looking ahead: the Göransson era and beyond.</strong> With four wins by age 41 (as of
          2026), Ludwig Göransson is on pace to potentially become the most-awarded composer in Oscar
          history within the next decade. But the category's history cautions against assuming linear
          trajectories — John Williams went 11 years between his third and fourth wins, and John Barry
          waited 17 years between his second and third. The increasing diversity of the Academy's
          music branch suggests that the coming decade will likely balance repeat recognition with
          continued discovery of new voices.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

